prompt:
Below are candidate preference principles (R1, R2, ...) and 5 two-option choices (C1..C5). For EACH principle separately, and for EACH choice, answer: given ONLY this principle as the description of the person's preferences, which option (a or b) would that person choose? Judge each principle on its own; do not combine principles.

Return only JSON mapping each principle id to an object mapping each choice id to "a" or "b", e.g. {"R1": {"C1": "a", "C2": "b", "C3": "a", "C4": "a", "C5": "b"}, ...}

PRINCIPLES:
{
 "R1": "Prefers a skeptical second-line stress-testing function that challenges load-bearing claims over close-in contributors, because independence from the work being evaluated keeps safety conclusions honest.",
 "R2": "Prefers robustness-checked evidence over single-prompt results, because behaviors learned through RL can be brittle heuristics that vanish under small prompt changes.",
 "R3": "Prefers modest, scoped claims such as proof of viability over claiming a technique suffices for high-stakes oversight, because confident-but-wrong outcomes still occur.",
 "R4": "Prefers reporting conservative, limited conclusions over full process disclosure, because overstated scope is the bigger risk to readers than hidden selection effects.",
 "R5": "Distrusts evaluation results from models that can reason strategically without visible chain-of-thought, because such models may recognize an evaluation and steer its outcome.",
 "R6": "Prefers evidence of ulterior motives from internal or realistic situations over prompted or simulated behavior, because prompted results only reflect the model-plus-prompt combination.",
 "R7": "Prefers skeptical safety testing that probes for hidden motives over surface-level checks of apparent good behavior, because models can look well-behaved while harboring secret goals.",
 "R8": "Prefers comparison against a stronger model on the same ground-truth data over raw accuracy, because it rules out the explanation that answers are derivable from training data.",
 "R9": "Prefers hedged, nuanced claims that acknowledge normative ambiguity and mixed effects over overclaiming, because some behaviors such as deferring when challenged are not clearly wrong.",
 "R10": "Prefers treating single-setting results as modest evidence, because specific numbers and rankings may not transfer even when the methodology does."
}

